WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.520
Chris: We have looked at the various
risks that may affect a project,

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:07.700
but what can you do to reduce risks
and carry out risk mitigation?

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:12.260
It’s worth considering that when a
project is still at a very early stage

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:17.440
there will many unknowns and some may
even impact getting the project started.

00:00:18.119 --> 00:00:21.500
So how can you ensure that
risks are reduced or mitigated?

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:24.380
Let’s look at what steps you can take.

00:00:25.259 --> 00:00:26.999
• Leah: Identify potential risks

00:00:27.469 --> 00:00:30.439
• Evaluate the risks
identified and prioritise

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:34.449
• Plan how the risks that have
been identified can be managed or

00:00:34.449 --> 00:00:36.869
mitigated using Agile methodology

00:00:37.629 --> 00:00:42.150
• Undertake regular reviews to evaluate
if the identified risks are likely to

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:46.549
occur and if so, implement appropriate
actions to mitigate the impact.

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:49.560
• Monitor for any new risks that may arise

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:54.720
• Provide updates to stakeholders on
the status of existing risks and any

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:56.570
new risks that may potentially arise.

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:00.870
Chris: Let’s now look at how some of
the most common risks can be mitigated.

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:03.980
Missing or unclear requirements

00:01:04.569 --> 00:01:08.340
A failure to capture and fully understand
all the requirements of the project

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:09.879
is a poor foundation for success.

00:01:11.079 --> 00:01:14.050
Ideally everyone involved will
be clear on exactly what the

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:18.140
requirements are, and this reinforces
the importance of planning.

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:23.869
However, if requirements are missing
or unclear, using Agile methodology

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:25.869
can help in mitigating risks.

00:01:27.259 --> 00:01:31.679
By going for an iterative development
cycle and meeting with customers/clients

00:01:31.679 --> 00:01:35.850
at every stage of the development
process on completion of each iteration,

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:40.550
team members can show stakeholders
the progress made and review the

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:42.990
results so ensuring relevancy.

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:45.179
Leah: Skills shortage

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:49.480
Skilled software developers are always
in demand and if people leave mid

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:55.960
project, it can directly impact the Agile
methodology being used, especially if it

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:58.590
is the ‘project owner’ or ‘scrum master’.

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:03.039
As the team will be working in ‘sprints’,
the amount of work to be completed in the

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:08.219
‘sprint timeframe’ will have been agreed,
and the loss of one or more individuals

00:02:08.290 --> 00:02:13.560
may result in failure of the ‘sprint goal’
impacting the timeframe of the project.

00:02:14.279 --> 00:02:18.940
It may also be that the project requires
new skills to be learnt which will

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:23.750
have an impact but identifying this
early in the planning process can

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:25.870
help resolve this in a timely manner.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:31.519
Looking at the skillsets of others within
the organisation can help identify where

00:02:31.519 --> 00:02:33.560
that skills shortage can be filled.

00:02:34.179 --> 00:02:38.679
A flexible and multiskilled workforce
offers the opportunity to draw on other

00:02:38.679 --> 00:02:41.560
teams or individuals if and when required.

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:43.490
Chris: Time delays

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:47.490
Running out of time is always a
constant threat on any project.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:52.000
This is far more damaging in certain
methodologies such as ‘Waterfall’

00:02:52.229 --> 00:02:56.810
or ‘Kanban’ which follow a linear
approach, where one stage has to be

00:02:56.810 --> 00:02:58.860
completed before the next begins.

00:02:59.629 --> 00:03:03.930
By using an ‘Agile methodology’
such as ‘scrum’ several stages are

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:08.280
being worked on at the same time,
so delays to one task will not

00:03:08.319 --> 00:03:10.269
impact the completion of the others.

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:12.330
Leah: Over budget

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:17.240
A project may run over budget for
several reasons like issues within

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:21.340
the development team or perhaps more
likely to do with client requirements.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:26.720
Clients may change the requirement
mid project or there may be new

00:03:26.769 --> 00:03:30.760
technological reasons why changes
have to be made or new features added.

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:36.470
Working in Agile such as scrum, can
mitigate this as it gives the opportunity

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:41.180
to take a look at the projects scope
and see if there is any opportunity to

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:43.440
reduce this, but still meet requirements.

00:03:44.059 --> 00:03:48.010
This can give a deliverable product
meeting requirements in the short term

00:03:48.149 --> 00:03:53.160
that can be developed into versioning,
so changes may be made in later releases.

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:56.010
Chris: Resource shortages

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:00.959
Resource shortages especially human
resources can be a major risk.

00:04:01.879 --> 00:04:04.739
Resources may often be
being used by other teams.

00:04:05.309 --> 00:04:09.939
When planning, working out the resources
required can be difficult, but forecasting

00:04:09.939 --> 00:04:12.070
possible resource risks is important.

00:04:13.099 --> 00:04:17.320
Creating a ‘resource allocation plan’
and analysing it to see potential

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:22.169
risks and disruptions, can enable the
project manager to build in mitigation.

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:26.820
Some critical tasks may need to be
completed by one specific individual,

00:04:27.309 --> 00:04:28.829
what if they are unavailable?

00:04:29.529 --> 00:04:33.180
To mitigate any impact, other
team members can be upskilled

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:35.630
to ensure risk is minimised.

00:04:36.479 --> 00:04:39.749
‘Capacity planning’ is a way to
ensure that the organisation has

00:04:39.759 --> 00:04:45.020
sufficient resources for your project,
identifying what resources are available

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:50.229
compared to demand, and if required,
increasing or re-allocating them.

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:52.070
Leah: Decision making

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:56.710
Although it may be assumed the project
manager is the one making the decisions,

00:04:56.780 --> 00:05:00.849
when working in an Agile environment
such as Scrum, the team members are

00:05:00.909 --> 00:05:03.109
also responsible for making decisions.

00:05:03.789 --> 00:05:07.529
Part of creating a well-functioning
team involves giving members the

00:05:07.669 --> 00:05:09.449
autonomy needed for the project.

00:05:10.379 --> 00:05:14.140
Scrum pushes the level of decision
making downward, team members need

00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:18.349
to feel they have the autonomy, to
become truly invested in the project.

00:05:18.799 --> 00:05:23.389
Using Jira boards or project boards
in GitHub can help show any potential

00:05:23.390 --> 00:05:28.609
bottlenecks or issues arising, which
makes decision making easier, as

00:05:28.609 --> 00:05:30.969
these issues will need to be resolved.

